Apple's <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/08/25/apple-announces-2026-mac-mini/">newly announced M6 and M5 Pro Mac mini models</a> still support up to three external displays, but there's now support for higher refresh rates, according to <a href="https://images.apple.com/mac-mini/specs/">Apple's published technical specifications</a>.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2024/10/Apple-Mac-mini-back-facing-ports-1.jpg" alt="" width="3840" height="2160" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-973621" /><br/>
Here's what's changed versus the M4 generation:<br/>
<ul><br/>
  <li>The M6 model runs two displays at up to 6K 60Hz or 4K 165Hz, up from 144Hz on M4, plus a third display at up to 5K 60Hz over Thunderbolt or 4K 60Hz over HDMI.</li><br/>
  <li>The M5 Pro model runs all three displays at up to 6K 60Hz or 4K 165Hz, up from 144Hz on the M4 Pro.</li><br/>
  <li>In a two-monitor setup, the M5 Pro model's second screen now supports 5K 120Hz or 4K 200Hz, whereas the M4 Pro listed 6K 60Hz or 4K 144Hz.</li><br/>
  <li>The M5 Pro model supports all three displays through one Thunderbolt port, while the M6 still supports two per port.</li><br/>
  <li>Single-display support is unchanged: Both chips support 8K 60Hz, 5K 120Hz, or 4K 240Hz.</li><br/>
</ul>As for port compatibility around the back, the M6 model has Thunderbolt 4 supporting DisplayPort 1.4, while the M5 Pro model has Thunderbolt 5 supporting DisplayPort 2.1. <br/>
<br/>
